\chapter{Tutorial: Backing claims with an agent}
\label{ch:tutorial-scholar-with-an-agent}

The other tutorials teach a person to drive Scholar.
This one teaches a person to \emph{delegate} the driving to an agent
and still stay in charge.
The [[backing-claims]] skill (a Claude Code skill) makes an agent back
every factual claim with a small two-sided literature review: it
searches for support and for refutation, reads the sources, records why
each one was picked, and leaves an audit trail a reader can follow.
Scholar is the tool the skill prefers, because Scholar records the
query, the providers and the decisions for it.
The agent never touches the TUI; it uses the non-interactive commands
only, and every one of them is available to the person too.

The object of learning is therefore the \emph{trail}, not the search:
what a claim session looks like after the agent has worked, and which
command reveals each part of it.
The learner runs each move of the skill's find--verify--record protocol
once, by hand, so that the same command in an agent's transcript is
recognisable later.
The arc follows the protocol's order:
\begin{enumerate}
\item check which databases are alive (step~1),
\item search in both directions under one named session (steps~2--3),
\item record a decision as the agent would, then confirm it as the
  human (steps~4--5),
\item reconstruct where a source came from and export the audit table
  (steps~6--7), and
\item name the protocol's required fields and the way to start the
  agent (steps~8--9).
\end{enumerate}
The invariant across steps~2--7 is the session name [[claim-literate]]:
one claim, one session, and every command reads or writes that one
record.
What varies is who is meant to run the command in real use---the agent
in steps~1--4 and~6--7, the person in step~5---and the prose of each
step says which.
That contrast is the point: the person sees that the agent's work and
their own review land in the same place.

