\section{Step 1: Check the providers first}

The skill's first instruction is to run [[scholar providers check]]
before any search and to record the result.
The reason is a failure mode that is invisible otherwise: a rejected
key (an expired Semantic Scholar key answering HTTP~403, say) makes
Scholar fall back or skip that database with only a warning, so a claim
that was \enquote{not found} may simply never have been looked for
there.
The step also asks for [[echo $?]] because the exit code is what an
agent's script gates on: it is~1 exactly when a \emph{configured}
provider fails, so a missing optional key is not a failure but a dead
key is.

The anchors are the report's title and the word [[ok]]; the learner's
provider set is unknown, so nothing else in the table is predictable.

<<providers check step>>=
# Check the providers first

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+providers\s+check\b
  - Provider Health
  - ok
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)echo\s+\$\?
hint: "The providers group has a verb that probes every database with one request; run it, then print the exit code with `echo $?`."
```

Run `scholar providers check`, then `echo $?`.

This is the agent's pre-flight. Every provider is probed with a
one-result request and reported as `ok`, `key rejected`, `rate limited`,
`http error`, `network error` or `not configured`. A database whose key
is dead would otherwise drop out of every search with only a warning,
and a claim that is "not found" would be no real negative.

The exit code is `1` exactly when a *configured* provider fails, so a
script can stop before it spends a whole session searching blind.
@

\section{Step 2: Search for support under a named session}

Backing a claim starts with a topic-driven search, and the skill asks
that every search of one claim run under one named session
([[-n <claim-slug>]]) so support and counter queries land in one
auditable record.
The output format [[bibtex+prov]] is the second thing the learner should
notice: each entry comes wrapped in a provenance comment block with
[[FOUND-VIA]] and [[DATE]] already filled from the query.
The remaining fields are [[TODO]] on purpose---[[CLAIM]], [[PICKED]],
[[QUOTE]] and [[VERIFIED]] can only be written after the agent has read
the source, and the block makes that debt visible.

The query is narrower than the one the earlier tutorials use because a
claim is narrower than a topic; the prose says so, so the learner does
not read the difference as arbitrary.
The anchors are the block delimiter and the [[FOUND-VIA]] label, which
appear for every entry regardless of what the provider returns.

<<support search step>>=
# Search for support under a named session

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+search\s+.*-n\s+claim-literate\b
  - mode: regex
    pattern: -f\s+bibtex\+prov\b
  - "% === provenance:"
  - "FOUND-VIA:"
hint: "Name the session after the claim with `-n claim-literate`, and ask for the `bibtex+prov` format so each entry comes with its provenance block."
```

Run:

```
scholar search "literate programming readability" -p openalex -l 5 \
    -n claim-literate -f bibtex+prov
```

Say the claim is "literate programming makes programs easier to read".
The query names the claim's concepts, not an author: discovery is
topic-driven, and a search that starts from expected names only finds
what one already believed.

`-n claim-literate` records the search in a session named after the
claim. `-f bibtex+prov` prints each entry under a provenance comment
block:

```
 % === provenance: knuth1984 ===
 % CLAIM: TODO
 % FOUND-VIA: scholar search 'literate programming readability' -p openalex --limit 5
 % PICKED: TODO
 % QUOTE (<loc>): TODO
 % VERIFIED: TODO
 % DATE: 2026-09-17
 @article{knuth1984, ...}
```

`FOUND-VIA` and `DATE` are filled in from the query. The `TODO` fields
are the agent's debt: they can only be written after reading the source.
@

\section{Step 3: Search against the claim, into the same session}

One agreeable source is confirmation, not backing.
The skill requires a counter-search for every load-bearing claim: a
query phrased to surface literature that refutes or qualifies it, run
on the same providers.
This step is the tutorial's central contrast.
The command is the same as in step~2 except for the query, and the
session name is deliberately unchanged, so when the learner then runs
[[scholar sessions show claim-literate]] the record lists both queries
under \enquote{Queries by provider}.
Query varies, session invariant: that is how one session becomes the
audit record for both directions of a claim.

The anchors are the two commands and the [[Queries by provider:]]
heading; the paper list is left unanchored because provider ranking
changes.

<<counter search step>>=
# Search against the claim, into the same session

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+search\s+.*criticism.*-n\s+claim-literate\b
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+show\s+claim-literate\b
  - "Queries by provider:"
hint: "Rerun the search with a query aimed at refutation, keeping `-n claim-literate` and the format, then show the session to see both queries listed."
```

Run the counter-search into the same session, then show the session:

```
scholar search "literate programming criticism limitations" -p openalex \
    -l 5 -n claim-literate -f bibtex+prov
scholar sessions show claim-literate
```

Only the query changed. The session name stayed, so `sessions show`
lists both searches under "Queries by provider" and the papers of both
in one pending list. Support and counter queries belong to the same
claim, so they belong to the same record.

The counter-search has three outcomes and the agent must record all of
them: refuting work found (soften the claim or cite both sides),
qualifying work found (narrow the claim's scope), or nothing found
(record the query and its emptiness). An unrun counter-search is not a
negative result.
@

\section{Step 4: Record a decision the way the agent does}

An agent that screens the hit list records its decisions with
[[scholar sessions decide --source llm]].
The flag is the design point of this step: such a decision is stored as
an \emph{unreviewed} LLM decision, and the TUI queues it for
confirmation instead of treating it as settled.
The learner sees the same thing in [[scholar llm status]], where the row
counts as \enquote{LLM (unreviewed)}.

The decision needs a DOI, and the learner has just seen [[sessions
show]] print one under every title as the paper id ([[doi:...]]), so the
step sends them back to that listing rather than to the search output.
This is also how an agent finds its selectors: [[show -f json]] carries
the same [[doi]] and [[paper_id]] fields.
The pattern accepts any DOI because the learner's result list is not
predictable.

<<llm decision step>>=
# Record a decision the way the agent does

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+decide\s+claim-literate\s+.*--source\s+llm\b
  - mode: regex
    pattern: --doi\s+10\.\S+
  - mode: regex
    pattern: marked kept
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+llm\s+status\s+claim-literate\b
hint: "Take a DOI from the `doi:` ids in `sessions show`, keep the paper with `sessions decide` and `--source llm --tag supports-claim`, then look at `scholar llm status`."
```

Pick a DOI from the session listing of the previous step (the `doi:`
line under a title; `scholar sessions show claim-literate` prints it
again) and keep that paper as the agent would:

```
scholar sessions decide claim-literate --source llm --keep \
    --doi <the DOI> --tag supports-claim
scholar llm status claim-literate
```

`--source llm` records an *unreviewed* decision: the status report
counts it under "LLM (unreviewed)", and the TUI would queue it for your
confirmation rather than treat it as final. The tag is one of the
skill's two-sided categories: `supports-claim`, `qualifies-claim`,
`adjacent-subtopic` or `off-topic-false-hit`.
@

\section{Step 5: Confirm it as the human}

The human's part of the loop is the same command without
[[--source]].
A human decision on a paper that carries an unreviewed LLM decision
marks it reviewed (and flags it as a changed example if the status
differs), which is exactly what pressing a key in the TUI would do.
Repeating the command with one flag removed makes the division of
labour concrete: the agent proposes, the person confirms, and both
actions write to the same session.
The learner then reads the effect in [[scholar llm status]], where the
count moves from unreviewed to reviewed.

<<human confirmation step>>=
# Confirm it as the human

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+decide\s+claim-literate\s+(?!.*--source)
  - mode: regex
    pattern: --doi\s+10\.\S+
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+llm\s+status\s+claim-literate\b
  - "LLM (reviewed)"
hint: "Repeat the decision for the same DOI without `--source`, then check the status report again."
```

Confirm the same paper yourself, then look at the status again:

```
scholar sessions decide claim-literate --keep --doi <the same DOI> \
    --tag supports-claim
scholar llm status claim-literate
```

Without `--source` the decision is a human one. Because the paper
already had an unreviewed LLM decision, yours marks it reviewed: the
count moves from "LLM (unreviewed)" to "LLM (reviewed)". Had you
discarded it instead, it would also be recorded as a changed decision,
which later classification rounds use as an example.

The agent proposes, you confirm, and both of you write to the same
session.
@

\section{Step 6: Reconstruct where a source came from}

Provenance must be reproducible: the skill's [[FOUND-VIA]] field is a
command that surfaces the paper again.
[[scholar prov found-via]] prints that line for one paper from the
session record, so the learner sees that the block of step~2 is not
special output of the search command but a view of what the session
stores.
The prose also names [[prov export]] and [[prov import]], the
round-trip between provenance blocks in a [[.bib]] file and Scholar's
notes, because that is how an agent's finished blocks get back into
Scholar; running them needs a [[.bib]] file the learner does not have
yet, so they are described rather than required.

<<found-via step>>=
# Reconstruct where a source came from

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+prov\s+found-via\s+
  - "FOUND-VIA:"
  - (session "claim-literate")
hint: "The prov group has a verb that prints the FOUND-VIA line for one paper; give it the DOI prefixed with `doi:`."
```

Run, with the DOI you decided on:

```
scholar prov found-via "doi:<the DOI>"
```

The output is the `FOUND-VIA` line for that paper, rebuilt from the
session record: the query, the provider and the session name. This is
what makes the provenance block of a search reproducible, and it is
available for any paper in any session, long after the search output
has scrolled away.

The other two `prov` verbs close the loop with a `.bib` file:
`scholar prov import refs.bib` pulls the provenance blocks an agent
wrote above each entry into Scholar's notes, and
`scholar prov export refs.bib` writes them back. You will meet them
when you have a `.bib` to hand.
@

\section{Step 7: Export the audit table}

When the claim belongs to a paper, the skill asks for a backing
appendix with the classified hit list as a table, generated by
[[scholar sessions export -f table]].
The step uses [[--bearing-only]] because that is the form the skill
prescribes: rows that bear on the claim (cited, supporting, qualifying)
are listed, the rest are counted in the caption.
The learner has one kept paper and the rest pending, so the table
will be short, but the file exists and its shape is right; the anchor
is the file name in the [[Exported:]] list.

<<audit table step>>=
# Export the audit table

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+export\s+claim-literate\s+.*-f\s+table\b
  - --bearing-only
  - claim-table.tex
hint: "Export the session in the `table` format with `--bearing-only`, `--lang en`, a `--label` and `-o claim-table`."
```

Run:

```
scholar sessions export claim-literate -f table --bearing-only \
    --lang en --label claim -o claim-table
```

`claim-table.tex` is a LaTeX fragment: one row per work with the
reason it was kept or excluded, ready to `\input` into the backing
appendix of a paper. `--bearing-only` lists only the rows that bear on
the claim and states the other counts in the caption.

In a real run the agent classifies the whole session first
(`scholar llm classify`), so every row carries a category and a
confidence; here most rows are still pending, so the table is short.
@

\section{Step 8: Name the required provenance fields}

The question steps close the tutorial by asking the learner to state,
rather than perform, what they have seen.
The skill's required fields are [[CLAIM]], [[FOUND-VIA]], [[PICKED]],
[[QUOTE]] and [[VERIFIED]]; [[DATE]] is recommended and [[COUNTER]] is
required for load-bearing claims.
The distractors [[ABSTRACT]] and [[RATING]] are chosen because they
are plausible things to record about a paper that the protocol
deliberately does not ask for: an abstract is not a verbatim supporting
quote, and a rating is not a written reason.
Picking them out is how the learner shows that the fields are about
\emph{auditability}, not about describing the paper.

<<provenance fields question step>>=
# Name the required provenance fields

```tutorial-step
kind: multi_select
options:
  - CLAIM
  - FOUND-VIA
  - PICKED
  - QUOTE
  - VERIFIED
  - ABSTRACT
  - RATING
answers:
  - CLAIM
  - FOUND-VIA
  - PICKED
  - QUOTE
  - VERIFIED
hint: "Five fields are required above every entry; each records something a reader could check. An abstract and a rating are not among them."
```

Which fields must every provenance block carry?

The block you saw in step 2 had `TODO` for the fields only a reader of
the source can fill: the claim the entry backs, why this source was
picked over the alternatives, a verbatim quote with its location, and
how the source was verified. `FOUND-VIA` was already filled from the
search. `DATE` is recommended, and `COUNTER` (the counter-search and
its outcome) is required for load-bearing claims.
@

\section{Step 9: Start the agent}

The final step turns the tutorial back into its purpose.
Everything so far was done by hand so that an agent's work is
recognisable; the way to get that work done is to load the skill and
hand over the claim.
The answer is a [[kind: input]] question rather than a shell step
because starting an agent is not something the tutorial shell can
verify, and because what matters is that the learner knows the skill's
name.
The regex therefore accepts any answer that names [[backing-claims]],
whether as a slash command or in a sentence.
The body summarises what the agent will do in the terms of steps~1--7,
so the learner can map the agent's transcript back onto this tutorial,
and it repeats the skill's own rule that the interactive TUIs are never
driven by an agent.

<<start the agent question step>>=
# Start the agent

```tutorial-step
kind: input
answers:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: backing-claims
hint: "Name the skill: it is the one about backing claims."
```

Type how you would start an agent on a claim in Claude Code.

Ask for the skill by name: `/backing-claims`, or "load the
backing-claims skill and back this claim: ...". Then give it the claim
as one sentence.

With the skill loaded, the agent does what you just did, for real:

- runs `scholar providers check` and records which databases are alive;
- searches for support and for refutation under one named session per
  claim, on the same providers, with `-f bibtex+prov`;
- reads each candidate source and captures a verbatim quote, screens
  the hits into supports, qualifies and refutes, and records its
  decisions with `sessions decide --source llm`;
- fills in the provenance blocks (`CLAIM`, `PICKED`, `QUOTE`,
  `VERIFIED`, `COUNTER`) and classifies and exports the session so
  the audit table can be generated.

You review its trail with the commands from this tutorial:
`sessions show`, `llm status`, `sessions decide` to confirm or
overturn, `prov found-via`, and `sessions export`.

The agent never drives the interactive parts of Scholar: `search
--review`, `sessions resume`, `snowball` and `tuxedo` are yours.
@
